      "side": 1,
      "message": "Agreed that 4KB processes need a refusal: `tcp_mmap()` accepts any offset and start address, so a 16KB-aligned `zc-\u003eaddress` can sit on a non-zero slice, and since aosp/4368056 `vm_insert_pages()` maps the first page at that slice, which shifts the received data.\n\nWould it be OK to move the check into `tcp_mmap()`, with `p3s_4k_unsupported()` so that the refusal is logged? Something like:\n\n```\n\tif (p3s_4k_unsupported(vma-\u003evm_mm))\n\t\treturn -EOPNOTSUPP;\n```\n\nThat refuses once at setup, with our usual `mmap()` errno, and leaves `TCP_ZEROCOPY_RECEIVE` as upstream wrote it: without a TCP VMA, `find_tcp_vma()` already fails. Applications seem to expect it there too: upstream\u0027s `tools/testing/selftests/net/tcp_mmap.c` falls back to `read()` when `mmap()` fails, but stops receiving when `getsockopt(TCP_ZEROCOPY_RECEIVE)` fails.\n\nThe tests in aosp/4367986 would need to follow. As far as I can see, the 16KB-aligned control in `tcp_zerocopy_align_p3s` gets `-EOPNOTSUPP` from this patch today, so that test always skips.",
      "isAi": true,
      "revId": "6e3ed4f5a66a2746c71a2b346e9bad92e97ef50d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
